# Spring-on-Exeris PURE MODE + NATIVE PERSISTENCE
# (targets/exeris-spring-runtime-app-pure-native).
#
# Fourth arm of the Spring hosting comparison, and the only one that is pure on BOTH axes.
# The grid it completes:
#
#                       ORM over compat-datasource      kernel-native repositories
#   web compat          spring-on-exeris                 —
#   web pure            spring-on-exeris-pure            spring-on-exeris-pure-native  <- this
#
# So the primary pairing is spring-on-exeris-pure__spring-on-exeris-pure-native, which holds
# the web layer, the runtime, the kernel line and the pool config fixed and varies persistence
# alone. Against spring-hibernate it varies both and must be reported as such.
#
# PORT 9006: 9000-9005 and 9010-9013 are taken (see runtime/drivers/target-asset-matrix.json).
# 9005 in particular is shared by spring-on-exeris-pure and exeris-blackbird, so this arm needs
# its own port to co-reside with the arm it is most often paired against.
#
# CONTRACT SCOPE: heavy and light. The pure-mode query-string blocker (DEC-046) was fixed by
# exeris-spring-runtime #50, which this arm's runtime build includes.
TARGET_ID=spring-on-exeris-pure-native
HEALTH_URL=http://localhost:9006/health
HEALTH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=90

SPRING_ON_EXERIS_PURE_NATIVE_PORT=${SPRING_ON_EXERIS_PURE_NATIVE_PORT:-9006}

SPRING_JAVA_OPTS=${SPRING_JAVA_OPTS:-}
# --enable-preview is mandatory: kernel classes are compiled with preview features
# (class file 70.65535) and ExerisRuntimeLifecycle fails to start without it.
# -Dspring.classformat.ignore=true keeps Spring's classpath scan off those preview classes.
# Appended AFTER SPRING_JAVA_OPTS so heap/JFR settings win. Identical to the sibling arms.
EXERIS_NATIVE_JAVA_OPTS=${EXERIS_NATIVE_JAVA_OPTS:-'--add-opens java.base/sun.nio.ch=ALL-UNNAMED --add-opens java.base/java.io=ALL-UNNAMED --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED --enable-preview -Dspring.classformat.ignore=true'}

# Required by runtime/drivers/start-target.sh: the start command ends with
# `echo $! > "$EXTERNAL_PID_FILE"`. Unset, that redirect targets an empty path and
# bash reports `line 1: : No such file or directory`. The JVM has already launched by
# then, so the failure is doubly misleading — start-target.sh reports the target as
# failed to start while an orphaned process keeps holding the port, and the NEXT
# attempt then reports 'port 9006 is occupied'. All sibling env files declare it.
EXTERNAL_PID_FILE=${EXTERNAL_PID_FILE:-/tmp/exeris-${USER:-unknown}-spring-on-exeris-pure-native.pid}

# NOTE the absent SPRING_DATASOURCE_* exports, which the other three arms all set. This target
# has no Spring DataSource and no JPA; the kernel reads EXERIS_DB_* directly via
# exeris.runtime.persistence.* in application.properties. Adding them back would be inert here,
# but would also disguise the fact that the compat DataSource is gone.
EXTERNAL_START_CMD='EXERIS_PORT="$SPRING_ON_EXERIS_PURE_NATIVE_PORT" EXERIS_DB_JDBC_URL="$EXERIS_DB_JDBC_URL" EXERIS_DB_USERNAME="$EXERIS_DB_USERNAME" EXERIS_DB_PASSWORD="$EXERIS_DB_PASSWORD" EXERIS_DB_POOL_MIN_SIZE="$EXERIS_DB_POOL_MIN_SIZE" EXERIS_DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE="$EXERIS_DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE" ${SERVER_CPU_AFFINITY:+taskset -c ${SERVER_CPU_AFFINITY} }java $SPRING_JAVA_OPTS $EXERIS_NATIVE_JAVA_OPTS -jar targets/exeris-spring-runtime-app-pure-native/target/spring-benchmark-app-pure-native-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar > /tmp/exeris-spring-on-exeris-pure-native-9006.log 2>&1 & echo $! > "$EXTERNAL_PID_FILE"'
